1
00:00:00,125 --> 00:00:02,627
I want you to tell me if any of these sound like you.

2
00:00:02,711 --> 00:00:06,589
One, you feel like you've been lied to about what a good life looks like.

3
00:00:06,715 --> 00:00:15,306
You're pursuing a goal that sounds nice when you mention it at dinner with grandma, or a goal that wouldn't make your parents think you're a disappointment, and you really don't see it going anywhere.

4
00:00:15,432 --> 00:00:24,315
If you ignore that feeling, you wake up 10 years later with a degree, a decent job, and a never-ending sense of despair that you'll never be able to chase your own dreams.

5
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:28,486
Two, you're productive, but not actually making progress toward anything.

6
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:35,660
You wake up early, you listen to podcasts, you journal, you lift, your calendar is full, but you don't really know where you're going.

7
00:00:35,910 --> 00:00:39,622
Give it five years and you'll still be figuring out what to do with your life.

8
00:00:39,706 --> 00:00:44,753
Three, you're ambitious, but stay quiet because it sounds embarrassing to your friends and family.

9
00:00:44,836 --> 00:00:47,672
You want a unique life, you don't want to fit in.

10
00:00:47,881 --> 00:00:51,968
But when people ask about your future, you water it down so nobody laughs at it.

11
00:00:52,093 --> 00:00:54,471
You get stuck performing mental masturbation.

12
00:00:54,596 --> 00:00:59,684
You feel special for wanting to be unique, but you still do the same thing as everyone else.

13
00:00:59,768 --> 00:01:02,979
Nobody wants to end up in any of those three situations.

14
00:01:03,188 --> 00:01:05,315
This video will change that for you.

15
00:01:05,482 --> 00:01:17,410
Because one of my favorite things to do, it seems like a stupid past time, but I love to study the habits, actions, and thought processes of both successful people and mediocre people.

16
00:01:17,452 --> 00:01:24,918
And that's all success really is, is doing less of what the mediocre people do and doing more of what the successful people do.

17
00:01:25,126 --> 00:01:29,422
In the next few videos on this channel will actually be dedicated to all of that.

18
00:01:29,422 --> 00:01:30,632
This is just the first one.

19
00:01:30,757 --> 00:01:44,479
So when we reverse engineer the habits, actions, and thoughts of people who have done the impossible like Elon Musk or Steve Jobs and any of the other greatest creatives, thinkers, and builders of the past, there is one pattern I've noticed between them all.

20
00:01:44,521 --> 00:01:46,481
Their goals are delusional.

21
00:01:46,564 --> 00:01:48,858
That's the key to their success.

22
00:01:48,942 --> 00:01:50,735
And that's exactly what we're going to go over.

23
00:01:50,735 --> 00:02:06,543
First, why average goals lead to mediocrity and failure, the psychology of delusional goals and why they work and why you need them, how to enter the same reality as ultra successful people, and of course how to achieve what most people think is impossible.

24
00:02:06,584 --> 00:02:08,044
So that's what we're going to start with.

25
00:02:08,086 --> 00:02:11,047
Why realistic goals lead to failure and mediocrity.

26
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:13,216
And you fall into this category by default.

27
00:02:13,216 --> 00:02:15,552
Everyone falls into this category by default.

28
00:02:15,593 --> 00:02:18,221
And I think it's best illustrated by an Elon Musk quote.

29
00:02:18,346 --> 00:02:26,271
"The most common error of a smart engineer is to optimize a thing that should not exist." So you have goals, even if you didn't set them.

30
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:28,481
That's just how the mind works.

31
00:02:28,565 --> 00:02:37,156
And if you become aware of this or understand how your mind works, you can quickly see why you or other people aren't able to stick to the goals that they set.

32
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:44,956
And we're not talking about some New Year's resolution or some shallow goal that you set because you looked in the mirror and felt bad about yourself.

33
00:02:44,956 --> 00:02:51,337
So you wanted to go to the gym or start eating healthy and you eat one healthy meal and then you quit immediately because you just forgot about it.

34
00:02:51,337 --> 00:02:55,425
Something more important stole your attention like something funny on your phone.

35
00:02:55,466 --> 00:03:02,015
Or in other words, because another unconscious goal was more important than the one you set and you're just not aware of it.

36
00:03:02,098 --> 00:03:06,060
And that unconscious goal is usually convenience and comfort.

37
00:03:06,144 --> 00:03:07,103
That's what you want.

38
00:03:07,145 --> 00:03:09,439
Or you just don't want to draw attention to yourself.

39
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:16,738
So you stop eating healthy or going to the gym because secretly and even without admitting to yourself, you don't want to stand out.

40
00:03:16,821 --> 00:03:19,574
So we're not talking about those kinds of goals.

41
00:03:19,616 --> 00:03:23,119
We're talking about how your mind is like a heat seeking missile.

42
00:03:23,244 --> 00:03:28,207
Every thought you have and every action you take is aiming toward a goal.

43
00:03:28,458 --> 00:03:29,959
It just makes sense, right?

44
00:03:29,959 --> 00:03:33,379
I'm looking toward this camera because I have the goal of creating a video.

45
00:03:33,463 --> 00:03:37,592
I may be tapping my foot because I have the goal of just not being bored.

46
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:39,761
It's every single little thing.

47
00:03:39,761 --> 00:03:44,599
Everything is goal directed or directed by stimuli, which we'll talk about in a second.

48
00:03:44,766 --> 00:03:52,023
But you have to understand that your mind is optimizing for a goal that you probably never chose and that you don't even know is there.

49
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:58,863
Or someone else assigned you the goal like go to school, get a job, retire at 65 because that's what you were raised to do.

50
00:03:58,863 --> 00:04:01,658
That's what you were conditioned to do and you never questioned it.

51
00:04:01,658 --> 00:04:07,205
And so those sit in the back of your head and silently guide every action that you take without you realizing it.

52
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:11,501
So I want you to actually take a second and think about that because this is massive.

53
00:04:11,584 --> 00:04:18,967
This could mean that the entire direction of your life and where you end up in life is completely out of your control or at least out of your awareness.

54
00:04:19,008 --> 00:04:23,888
Every single thing you think or do is aiming toward a goal.

55
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:25,598
Do not forget about that.

56
00:04:25,682 --> 00:04:26,766
So here's how it goes.

57
00:04:26,808 --> 00:04:27,976
First you're born.

58
00:04:28,184 --> 00:04:30,311
Your first goal is survival.

59
00:04:30,395 --> 00:04:35,274
And then your parents have been thinking about what goals they want you to pursue for quite some time now.

60
00:04:35,316 --> 00:04:39,612
They want what's best for you so to say but really they want what's best for them.

61
00:04:39,612 --> 00:04:42,949
They want you to be a respectable young adult that doesn't embarrass them.

62
00:04:43,116 --> 00:04:49,956
And then your parents teach you how to walk, how to communicate and then eventually what their version of success for you looks like.

63
00:04:49,998 --> 00:04:56,587
Which is usually the goals that society passed down to them unless your parents questioned them or your parents were high agency.

64
00:04:56,629 --> 00:04:58,965
So go to school, get a job, praise your God.

65
00:04:59,007 --> 00:04:59,882
And so that's what you do.

66
00:04:59,882 --> 00:05:03,928
You go to school and then you go to college because that's just what's next.

67
00:05:03,928 --> 00:05:05,263
You don't really make that decision.

68
00:05:05,263 --> 00:05:07,056
That's the only decision you can make.

69
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:10,643
And then you get the job, you hit the milestones and you check the boxes.

70
00:05:10,768 --> 00:05:20,778
And then after almost a quarter of your life you start to wonder if this was what you wanted in the first place, which is kind of not cool.

71
00:05:20,862 --> 00:05:22,113
A quarter of your life is gone.

72
00:05:22,196 --> 00:05:27,910
And so the default state is having average goals and just going with the flow.

73
00:05:27,952 --> 00:05:31,664
And if you understand entropy, you understand how disastrous this can be.

74
00:05:31,664 --> 00:05:35,209
And entropy illustrates that systems trend toward disorder.

75
00:05:35,293 --> 00:05:41,716
Now applied to the mind, psychic entropy, psychic isn't some fancy word like you're moving water bottles with your mind.

76
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:43,634
It literally just means of the mind.

77
00:05:43,634 --> 00:05:44,385
It's the mind.

78
00:05:44,469 --> 00:05:50,600
So psychic entropy means that your mind and therefore your life drifts toward disorder and chaos.

79
00:05:50,683 --> 00:05:52,101
And you can see this everywhere.

80
00:05:52,143 --> 00:05:55,271
This is just what happens if you don't water a plant, it dies.

81
00:05:55,396 --> 00:05:59,150
If you don't clean your room, it slowly gets more disorganized with time.

82
00:05:59,567 --> 00:06:05,364
If you don't maintain habits of personal development, your mind slowly devolves into chaos.

83
00:06:05,406 --> 00:06:11,120
And so this is what happens on the default path is you have these average goals that you didn't choose, that you didn't set.

84
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:14,207
You don't put any effort into them because you don't actually care about them.

85
00:06:14,290 --> 00:06:16,751
And then you eventually find yourself in a rut.

86
00:06:16,918 --> 00:06:18,169
Everyone experiences that.

87
00:06:18,169 --> 00:06:20,171
That's why this is such an evergreen video.

88
00:06:20,171 --> 00:06:23,424
This shouldn't be a thing like you should want better for yourself.

89
00:06:23,466 --> 00:06:25,802
But that's also just the nature of reality.

90
00:06:25,802 --> 00:06:34,393
And I do think it's there for a reason, because at some point, a system becomes unstable and a new, more ordered structure must emerge.

91
00:06:34,435 --> 00:06:45,279
So when you think of water that is just pouring out of a faucet, let's say into a bathtub, eventually it gets so chaotic that a whirlpool starts to form a beautiful ordered whirlpool.

92
00:06:45,446 --> 00:06:51,953
So in your life, you hit a point where the lower part of you wants to stay the same and the higher part wants something more.

93
00:06:52,036 --> 00:06:53,871
And it feels like you're being ripped in half.

94
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:56,916
That is a signal to completely turn your life around.

95
00:06:56,958 --> 00:07:04,715
That's what my next newsletter is actually going to be about, is kind of how to launch out of a very bad position in your life, because that's the best time to do it.

96
00:07:04,757 --> 00:07:11,180
So the reason most average and realistic goals lead to mediocrity and failure is because of how you choose them.

97
00:07:11,264 --> 00:07:25,236
First, you look at what you think is possible or what you know is possible and what you know at this point in your life is really only told to you by your parents or your teachers or what other average people do.

98
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:36,914
And then from those possible goals, you water it down a bit so that people won't laugh at you for trying to improve yourself because whenever you try to improve yourself, crabs in the bucket mentality, all of your friends are gonna be like,

99
00:07:36,914 --> 00:07:39,000
Oh, you should drink this beer.

100
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,877
You shouldn't stick to your health goals.

101
00:07:41,043 --> 00:07:43,171
Like Oh, come on, just lighten up, live a little bit.

102
00:07:43,171 --> 00:07:45,548
And so you want to avoid that because you know it's going to happen.

103
00:07:45,548 --> 00:07:48,426
So you water down the goals and don't take them seriously in the first place.

104
00:07:48,467 --> 00:07:55,933
And a goal only feels realistic if it fits the life that your parents, teachers and friends already expected you to have.

105
00:07:56,017 --> 00:07:58,311
In other words, your mind is a cage.

106
00:07:58,394 --> 00:08:05,443
And if you keep choosing to live in that cage, you'll never have the chance to notice a potentially life changing opportunity.

107
00:08:05,443 --> 00:08:08,237
That's what a lot of this video is about is about noticing.

108
00:08:08,446 --> 00:08:12,074
And if you don't notice that opportunity, there's no way you can act on it.

109
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:20,041
And again, your life just slowly gets worse and worse and worse until the pain becomes great, that you either get crushed or you finally decide to act on it.

110
00:08:20,082 --> 00:08:21,375
So delusional goals.

111
00:08:21,417 --> 00:08:22,043
What are they?

112
00:08:22,084 --> 00:08:22,960
Why do they matter?

113
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:24,587
Are they actually delusional?

114
00:08:24,670 --> 00:08:27,590
We'll start with a quote to transition from Maxwell Maltz.

115
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:37,475
The man who conceives himself to be a failure type person will find some way to fail in spite of all his good intentions or willpower, even if the opportunity is literally dumped in his lap.

116
00:08:37,516 --> 00:08:45,650
The person who conceives himself to be a victim of injustice, one who was meant to suffer will invariably find circumstances to verify his opinions.

117
00:08:45,733 --> 00:08:52,156
Now, another thing I like to think about is why humans are unique or why humans are different from animals and humans.

118
00:08:52,240 --> 00:09:01,874
They're not unique in the sense that they have memory or a different type of consciousness than animals or a more refined intelligence than animals, even though those play a role.

119
00:09:01,999 --> 00:09:06,587
They're unique because they constantly simulate the future to steer the present.

120
00:09:06,754 --> 00:09:14,220
You think about the future more than you do the past, because usually you aren't really thinking about the past.

121
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:19,809
The future is what causes your present behavior.

122
00:09:20,059 --> 00:09:22,728
Again, all behavior is mostly goal directed.

123
00:09:22,937 --> 00:09:27,692
So contrary to, I guess, popular belief is your actions do not create the future.

124
00:09:27,692 --> 00:09:30,611
You create the future first and your actions follow.

125
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:37,785
So all of this falls under the umbrella of selective attention, meaning your brain either gets pulled by stimuli or directed by a goal.

126
00:09:38,077 --> 00:09:46,294
And when you focus on one thing, that means by definition, you're not focused on the 99.99 percent of other things you could be focusing on.

127
00:09:46,419 --> 00:09:51,591
Your attention is goal directed rather than tossed around like a ragdoll looking for the next cheap dopamine hit.

128
00:09:51,674 --> 00:09:55,344
That's the important part because delusional goals force this.

129
00:09:55,386 --> 00:10:02,268
If you're focused on one overarching delusional goal and only focused on that thing, then everything else is noise.

130
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:06,314
So I want you to imagine two different people with drastically different goals.

131
00:10:06,439 --> 00:10:10,067
Person one, Tommy, has the same goal as almost everyone else.

132
00:10:10,067 --> 00:10:18,159
Go to school, get an education, get a decent job so he can finally afford a nice apartment and acquire enough material status symbols to attract a mate.

133
00:10:18,242 --> 00:10:21,454
Person two, Johnny, has a goal that seems impossible.

134
00:10:21,495 --> 00:10:28,419
Build a content business that makes 100K a month in six months as one example that I guess many in my audience can relate with.

135
00:10:28,711 --> 00:10:34,800
Now will Tommy ever have the possibility of making more money than what a decent job would pay him?

136
00:10:35,176 --> 00:10:40,097
Will Tommy ever learn something outside of school that allows him to pursue a more lucrative opportunity?

137
00:10:40,097 --> 00:10:43,517
No, because that's not what his attention is selecting for.

138
00:10:43,559 --> 00:10:51,192
He could come across life changing information and his mind will consider it noise just because it's not necessary to achieve the goal he's pursuing.

139
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:54,612
Johnny, on the other hand, cannot think like the average person.

140
00:10:54,737 --> 00:11:00,076
To achieve that delusional goal, he realizes that most actions he's taking are completely useless.

141
00:11:00,201 --> 00:11:04,914
So a properly set delusional goal pushes your brain into the unknown.

142
00:11:05,081 --> 00:11:08,000
Everything you knew before is off the table, you can't use it.

143
00:11:08,125 --> 00:11:11,253
And what this does is it accelerates neuroplasticity.

144
00:11:11,420 --> 00:11:18,260
Consistency reinforces pathways in your brain, but novelty and challenge stimulate learning and change faster.

145
00:11:18,344 --> 00:11:27,645
So flipping the switch and going all in on a goal throws your brain into an environment where it has no choice but to rewire fast and the new behavior becomes your new baseline.

146
00:11:27,770 --> 00:11:38,906
And even better, many have experienced this in short bursts of their life is that this is when the five intrinsic drivers of flow and obsession come into play, which create a blissful quality of life.

147
00:11:38,989 --> 00:11:40,032
I mean, it's the flow state.

148
00:11:40,032 --> 00:11:42,118
It's the peak human experience.

149
00:11:42,243 --> 00:11:49,667
And those five drivers are one, curiosity, which is the desire to explore the unknown, learn how to change and fill knowledge gaps.

150
00:11:49,667 --> 00:11:55,089
And this results in good dopamine from novelty and norepinephrine, which heightens attention preparing you to learn.

151
00:11:55,214 --> 00:11:56,507
The second is passion.

152
00:11:56,549 --> 00:12:00,511
So an intense enthusiasm is built for the path that allows you to change your life.

153
00:12:00,761 --> 00:12:03,639
This results in more good dopamine and norepinephrine.

154
00:12:03,848 --> 00:12:08,686
The next is purpose, which is the feeling that your actions contribute to something larger than yourself.

155
00:12:08,811 --> 00:12:12,189
Achieving goals results in more dopamine and reinforces behavior.

156
00:12:12,273 --> 00:12:15,192
Serotonin stems from significance and belonging.

157
00:12:15,317 --> 00:12:17,236
Oxytocin stems from connection.

158
00:12:17,319 --> 00:12:30,916
The next is autonomy, which is the desire to direct your own life and work, to control your choices, actions and environment, which results in yet again more dopamine and a reduction in cortisol, the stress from feeling put in a box, allowing for creative decision making.

159
00:12:31,167 --> 00:12:34,462
And then mastery, the process of learning and growing, is its own reward.

160
00:12:34,503 --> 00:12:38,549
This results in sustainable good dopamine that keeps you in the game, keeps you playing.

161
00:12:38,757 --> 00:12:49,560
And so that's kind of what we're aiming for here is you throw yourself into the unknown, you become curious, and maybe you become curious before enough to throw yourself into the unknown, and then you generate passion, right?

162
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:53,314
You're not passionate about things until you've invested time and energy into them.

163
00:12:53,314 --> 00:12:55,983
You can't find your passion because you haven't done anything.

164
00:12:56,066 --> 00:13:00,362
You have to stick it out into the unknown until you become passionate about something.

165
00:13:00,446 --> 00:13:01,906
And so many people get this wrong.

166
00:13:01,906 --> 00:13:05,743
Like you don't have to be passionate about some fancy-pantsy artistic thing.

167
00:13:05,784 --> 00:13:08,037
You can become passionate about literally anything.

168
00:13:08,162 --> 00:13:16,045
As long as you dive deep enough into it and learn enough about it so that you can kind of nerd out about it, that's where passion starts to come into play.

169
00:13:16,045 --> 00:13:25,095
And then once you can tie that passion to a sense of purpose and eventually do your own thing with it, so autonomy and mastery, then that's the best way to live.

170
00:13:25,137 --> 00:13:27,598
That's what I'm all about with the things that I teach.

171
00:13:27,681 --> 00:13:32,269
And so this all sounds great, but I can hear your objection because I've faced the objection before myself.

172
00:13:32,353 --> 00:13:38,484
And that objection is, "But Dan, I've tried setting a goal like this before, and I didn't feel any of this.

173
00:13:38,484 --> 00:13:39,693
I didn't feel motivated.

174
00:13:39,693 --> 00:13:43,155
I just set the goal." And yes, that's exactly what we're trying to avoid.

175
00:13:43,155 --> 00:13:45,032
Your goal needs gravity.

176
00:13:45,115 --> 00:13:46,575
It needs to pull you toward it.

177
00:13:46,617 --> 00:13:51,205
It needs to be so strong that everything else in your life just becomes stupid and pointless.

178
00:13:51,288 --> 00:13:52,373
And that's difficult to do.

179
00:13:52,373 --> 00:13:55,918
That's why this is going to be a video series rather than just one video.

180
00:13:56,001 --> 00:14:00,297
So for now, we just have to learn how to properly set a delusional goal.

181
00:14:00,422 --> 00:14:02,841
And in reality, we're not setting a goal.

182
00:14:02,883 --> 00:14:05,636
What we're doing is we're entering a new reality.

183
00:14:05,678 --> 00:14:06,804
And that sounds woo-woo.

184
00:14:06,845 --> 00:14:09,014
That sounds weird, but that's what you're doing.

185
00:14:09,056 --> 00:14:13,018
And so we want to enter the same reality as ultra successful people.

186
00:14:13,060 --> 00:14:18,190
And a quote that I love by Anthony D'Amelio is that, "Every great idea starts out as blasphemy.

187
00:14:18,357 --> 00:14:25,573
Everyone is going to think you're crazy because that idea scares them." And I think the best way of illustrating this is with Steve Jobs.

188
00:14:25,698 --> 00:14:32,329
So Steve Jobs would walk into a room, state an impossible deadline or an impossible product as if it were already decided.

189
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:46,343
He'd say, "We'll ship it by January and it will do this with such total conviction that the room of engineers who knew it was impossible would leave believing it and then build toward it like it was simply the plan." This was Steve Jobs' reality distortion field.

190
00:14:46,385 --> 00:14:53,434
Most people would dismiss it as charisma or manipulation or him being an asshole when it was simply him re-aiming the room's attention.

191
00:14:53,559 --> 00:15:00,941
Steve Jobs refused to be pulled by the stimuli of "this timeline can't possibly work" and overrode it with a future target.

192
00:15:01,108 --> 00:15:06,155
Every brain in the room started selecting for a path instead of selecting for reasons it wouldn't work.

193
00:15:06,238 --> 00:15:07,323
And that's just the thing.

194
00:15:07,364 --> 00:15:09,867
Most people have a misunderstanding of what a goal is.

195
00:15:09,867 --> 00:15:12,911
A goal is not a destination that you hit.

196
00:15:12,953 --> 00:15:15,748
It's not climbing up the mountain and reaching the top.

197
00:15:15,789 --> 00:15:17,708
A goal is a point of view.

198
00:15:17,875 --> 00:15:23,005
Because when you're at the top of the mountain, now you have a completely different way of viewing everything underneath.

199
00:15:23,088 --> 00:15:24,923
It doesn't matter that you reach the top.

200
00:15:25,007 --> 00:15:28,010
Like you're going to go home and go to bed the same as you do every night.

201
00:15:28,093 --> 00:15:29,219
It's just not that important.

